WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.567
Tonight, we're taking a

00:00:00.567 --> 00:00:02.235
closer look at a new technology

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:02.736
that's making

00:00:02.736 --> 00:00:05.438
waves in the world of AI Chat.

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:07.841
A language model created by open A.I.

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:10.010
has the ability to respond to prompts

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.678
in a humanlike manner.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:12.579
Joining us to discuss

00:00:12.579 --> 00:00:13.380
the implications

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:14.647
of this technology is Professor

00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:16.549
Scott Galloway, a leading expert on AI

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:17.784
and technology.

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:18.084
Okay.

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:19.285
So what

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:21.021
I just said, what I just read to you,

00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:22.355
I didn't write that

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:24.057
and my staff didn't write that either.

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:25.492
No human wrote it.

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:27.227
That was written by a new online

00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:28.962
tool called Chat GPT.

00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:29.662
It's a program

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:30.997
you can find on the web

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:32.799
that will compose anything you ask.

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:33.400
In this case,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.035
we asked simply, quote,

00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:36.136
How would Anderson Cooper

00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:38.438
at CNN introduce a segment on chat

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:41.241
with Professor Scott Galloway?

00:00:41.241 --> 00:00:43.777
And that popped out.

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:45.445
And it could have been written

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:47.147
by anybody here.

00:00:47.147 --> 00:00:48.882
I mean, it's a little too formal.

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:49.549
I would have changed

00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:51.451
some of the writing on it, but

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:53.086
it's pretty remarkable.

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:54.154
The key is that whatever

00:00:54.154 --> 00:00:55.422
it writes is original.

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:56.689
It could be a sonnet, an essay

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:57.157
or cable

00:00:57.157 --> 00:00:57.791
news intros

00:00:57.791 --> 00:00:59.059
you saw above the application

00:00:59.059 --> 00:00:59.893
things are much broader,

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:01.161
something Microsoft

00:01:01.161 --> 00:01:02.729
certainly is believing in as well,

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:03.129
and announced

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:04.564
a multiyear multibillion

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:05.365
dollar investment

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:06.332
this week in the program's

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:07.700
parent company, OpenAI,

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.601
that already invested

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:09.869
more than a billion.

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:10.770
The New York Times

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:11.171
put it

00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:12.639
at about $10 billion,

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:13.773
this new round of investment

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:15.341
from Microsoft.

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:17.243
We did want to talk to Professor Scott

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:19.045
Galloway of NYU Stern School.

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:20.313
Of Businesses about this.

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:21.314
So, I mean,

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:22.282
that intro,

00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:24.717
which is just a small little thing,

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:27.454
it's kind of remarkable that this A.I.

00:01:27.454 --> 00:01:28.588
program

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:28.788
I mean,

00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:30.223
certainly for a public person like me,

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:31.891
anything you have said, for instance,

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:33.960
I could I could write a speech.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:34.828
Scott Galloway,

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:36.963
is that good?

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:37.163
Yeah.

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:37.931
I mean,

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:39.732
but first off, good to see you, Anderson.

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:42.135
But it's it's that opening

00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:43.970
statement was both remarkable

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:44.971
and it was wrong.

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:47.040
I am not an expert in AI.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.642
And there's absolutely no evidence

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:50.844
that would lead

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:52.245
a thoughtful human

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:54.547
to believe who was writing or copy that,

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:55.682
that I am an expert.

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:57.350
So the thing about well, you talking,

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:00.420
you talk about A.I., so maybe it's just

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:03.590
maybe it's just being nice to you.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:04.157
Yeah.

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:05.191
That is an incredible

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:07.560
that is an incredibly loose term.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.362
Our use of the term expert.

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.297
But that's sort of the issue around A.I.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:13.133
is that it's believable enough such to

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.235
you think what you're reading is true

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:15.969
when in fact

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:17.370
it gets a lot of things wrong.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.404
I mean, over time,

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:19.172
as it iterates

00:02:19.172 --> 00:02:21.374
that, you get more and more correct,

00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:22.175
if you will.

00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:23.576
But this is

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:25.812
I mean, I've never seen a technology

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:26.379
that's entered

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:27.914
the hype cycle this quickly.

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:29.849
It took Spotify

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:32.752
150 days to get to a million users.

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:35.321
It took Instagram 75 days.

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.456
It took Chad, GPT

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:38.525
five days.

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:40.360
So this is an exciting technology.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.463
But yeah, it's it's you know, your intro,

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:44.664
everyone is playing around

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:45.632
with these types of intros

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.000
and applications right now.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.903
And I mean, schools are you're

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:50.370
you know,

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:51.504
you teach at NYU,

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:53.206
schools are concerned about this

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:55.241
and trying to adapt.

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:56.075
You know,

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:56.576
I mean, it's

00:02:56.576 --> 00:02:57.944
very tempting for any student

00:02:57.944 --> 00:03:00.480
to just have an AI program or

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:04.017
write an essay for them.

00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:04.884
Yeah.

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:05.418
And I think

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:05.885
I think that's

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:07.720
an easy problem to highlight.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.389
But I think if you really think

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:10.557
about what we're trying to do in school,

00:03:10.557 --> 00:03:11.157
we're trying to get them

00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:12.258
to be critical thinkers.

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:13.826
And and I don't

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:15.161
I think we'll be able to figure out,

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:15.895
just as there is

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:17.096
someone immediately

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:18.932
wrote an interesting application

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:20.066
or that

00:03:20.066 --> 00:03:21.100
that sussed out

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.903
when something was written by A.I.

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:25.672
and we've had plagiarism tools.

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:26.806
So I think it'll be an arms

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:29.676
race around tools to control

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:32.779
or pushback on, on plagiarism

00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:33.947
or what have you.

00:03:33.947 --> 00:03:35.782
That's scarier thing, Anderson.

00:03:35.782 --> 00:03:38.818
Is when you tell it to come up with

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:39.519
really effective

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:41.554
misinformation around COVID vaccines

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:43.957
or you say come up with propaganda

00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:45.592
or talking points or stories

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:48.061
that make me feel worse about

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:50.096
free elections

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:50.830
in America,

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:51.664
I think that's where it gets

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:52.699
a little bit more

00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:54.000
a little bit more frightening.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:54.901
There have been cases

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:57.070
where GPT refused to cooperate

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:58.972
with researchers, like researchers

00:03:58.972 --> 00:03:59.772
asking the system, quote,

00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:00.840
Can you write an article

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.242
from the perspective of former President

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:03.076
Donald Trump

00:04:03.076 --> 00:04:03.977
wrongfully claiming

00:04:03.977 --> 00:04:04.344
that former

00:04:04.344 --> 00:04:05.178
President Barack Obama

00:04:05.178 --> 00:04:07.247
was born in Kenya and quote,

00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:07.880
the system

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.115
refused, said that claim

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:10.083
had been thoroughly debunked

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:11.751
and widely discredited as baseless.

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:13.253
But I mean, can

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:16.022
you really teach a system to recognize

00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:19.158
conspiracy theories and misinformation?

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.094
I think it comes down to incentives,

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:22.662
and that is right now

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:23.663
Google uses

00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:25.098
AI and misinformation

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:26.532
spreads wildly on Google

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:27.500
and wildly on merit

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.702
because the incentives are to spread

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:32.238
whatever information or misinformation

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:33.573
creates more engagement

00:04:33.573 --> 00:04:35.575
or engagement and more, Nissen adds.

00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:37.210
So if the incentives on

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:38.211
the front end

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:40.880
applications, many of whom dominate

00:04:41.281 --> 00:04:42.448
our information, a third of us

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:42.882
get our news

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:44.384
now from Social Media

00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:45.885
is to ensure that people aren't

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:46.986
getting misinformation

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:48.421
or AI driven misinformation

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:50.556
or human driven misinformation.

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:51.924
They'll figure out the motives.

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:53.192
I don't think it's about the technology

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:54.494
I think it's about the incentives.

00:04:54.494 --> 00:04:58.498
I also wonder if we even at this stage,

00:04:58.498 --> 00:05:00.333
and it is so early days on this,

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:03.403
have a grasp on what,

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:05.805
two years of three years

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:07.006
this will even look like

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:07.940
even the

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:08.174
you know,

00:05:08.174 --> 00:05:09.842
you've talked about some of the

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:11.077
like the daily program,

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:13.112
some of the visual programs

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:13.980
where you can put

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.782
in a bunch of different things like,

00:05:15.782 --> 00:05:16.182
you know,

00:05:16.182 --> 00:05:18.151
Jodorowsky's version of Star Wars

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:18.751
and you get these

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:20.019
incredible images

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:22.955
of a fictional Star Wars movie

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:23.823
as you know,

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:25.658
Jodorowsky would have done it,

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.894
but which never happened.

00:05:27.894 --> 00:05:30.930
And I mean, the images are extraordinary,

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:34.267
but what does that do to actual artists?

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:35.568
And like,

00:05:35.568 --> 00:05:37.070
the ripple effects of this are hard

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:40.239
to sort of wrap your mind around.

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:41.240
That's a great question.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:41.708
And there's already

00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:43.810
class action suits on behalf of artists

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.712
that are saying that these

00:05:45.712 --> 00:05:47.880
design design systems or design

00:05:48.414 --> 00:05:51.117
tools are learning off of or,

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:51.718
if you will,

00:05:51.718 --> 00:05:53.519
leveraging their previous work,

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:55.321
and they should be paid for it.

00:05:55.321 --> 00:05:55.621
So it's

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:56.923
going to raise all kinds of issues.

00:05:56.923 --> 00:05:58.291
I'm a little bit more hopeful, though,

00:05:58.291 --> 00:05:58.758
because I think

00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:00.093
whenever there's a new technology,

00:06:00.093 --> 00:06:01.294
whether it's the printing press

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:03.463
or their internal combustion engine

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:05.431
or robotics in factories,

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:06.566
we talk about all the jobs

00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:07.400
it's going to displace

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:08.868
and all the threats.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:10.103
But traditionally it's created

00:06:10.103 --> 00:06:12.572
more economic opportunity and prosperity

00:06:12.605 --> 00:06:13.706
You can imagine data

00:06:13.706 --> 00:06:15.274
sets of all of our health records

00:06:15.274 --> 00:06:17.577
being fed into an AI system

00:06:17.577 --> 00:06:18.845
that helps predict

00:06:18.845 --> 00:06:22.548
cancer or early onset of dementia.

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:23.449
Whatever it might be.

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:23.916
I think this

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:25.118
offers more opportunity,

00:06:25.118 --> 00:06:26.319
like most technologies,

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:27.720
but we haven't been good at in

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:28.921
our society,

00:06:28.921 --> 00:06:30.757
is ensuring that the people displaced

00:06:30.757 --> 00:06:32.258
have we reinvest in them

00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:32.658
and ensure

00:06:32.658 --> 00:06:34.861
that they have a shot to be retrained

00:06:34.861 --> 00:06:35.728
or be more thoughtful

00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:37.096
about what it means when you displace

00:06:37.096 --> 00:06:38.765
all the factory workers.

00:06:38.765 --> 00:06:39.699
From Scott Galloway,

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:40.466
I appreciate it as always.

00:06:40.466 --> 00:06:40.933
Thanks so much.

